WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:25.099
A

00:00:25.099 --> 00:00:30.710
hundred years ago, Egyptologist James Henry
Breasted described Hatshepsut as “the first

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:34.430
great woman in history of whom we are informed.”

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:40.090
This is particularly ironic seeing as how
one or more pharaohs who came after her tried

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:44.020
to have her erased from history through damnatio
memoriae.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.730
Maybe they hated her, maybe they resented
her success, or maybe they simply wanted a

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.240
return to tradition.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:56.370
Thankfully, they did not succeed, which means
that today we can bring you the story of Hatshepsut,

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:00.370
the woman who became a pharaoh.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:04.710
To Be a Female Pharaoh

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.610
Hatshepsut was born circa 1508 BC.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:13.759
She was part of Egypt’s 18th Dynasty which
was founded by Ahmosis I and became notable

00:01:13.759 --> 00:01:14.759
for several reasons.

00:01:14.759 --> 00:01:20.100
For starters, it also included other well-known
historical figures such as Tutankhamun and

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.180
Nefertiti.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:26.619
More importantly, though, it was the first
dynasty of the so-called New Kingdom, a period

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:30.679
in ancient Egyptian history which is considered
one of its “Golden Ages.”

00:01:30.679 --> 00:01:34.159
Some even hail it as the most prosperous time
for the empire.

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:39.140
This is debatable - others could argue that
the Old Kingdom which was defined by the construction

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:45.369
of the pyramids represented the true halcyon
days of Egypt - but one thing that’s certain

00:01:45.369 --> 00:01:49.819
is that, after the New Kingdom ended, the
empire experienced steady decline and would

00:01:49.819 --> 00:01:52.380
never again reach the same heights.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:56.609
This dynasty is also known as the Thutmosid
Dynasty because several of the first pharaohs

00:01:56.609 --> 00:02:00.469
were named Tuthmosis, or Thutmose, however
you prefer.

00:02:00.469 --> 00:02:06.759
Hatshepsut herself was the daughter of Tuthmosis
I, stepsister (and wife) of Tuthmosis II,

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:09.560
and stepmother of Tuthmosis III.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:14.799
Speaking of names, we already gave a brief
rundown on the naming conventions for pharaohs

00:02:14.799 --> 00:02:19.390
in our Tutankhamun video which you can check
out if you haven’t seen yet.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:22.780
Basically, the full royal titulary included
five separate names.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:28.170
Our pharaoh today typically went by Hatshepsut
which was part of her personal name, but she

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.379
was also often referred to by her throne name
of Maatkare.

00:02:32.379 --> 00:02:38.099
Hatshepsut is sometimes identified as the
first female pharaoh and this is not really

00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:39.099
true.

00:02:39.099 --> 00:02:42.260
She is certainly the first on whom we have
good information and you can make a strong

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:47.720
case that she was the greatest female pharaoh,
but she was not the first.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:52.180
Unfortunately, we cannot say with accuracy
who was the first because there is a lot of

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:55.370
uncertainty surrounding the rulers of the
earliest dynasties.

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:59.739
There were plenty of female regents who ruled
as guardians of child monarchs until they

00:02:59.739 --> 00:03:00.739
came of age.

00:03:00.739 --> 00:03:05.079
However, as early as the First Dynasty, it
is possible that queens such as Merneith or

00:03:05.079 --> 00:03:10.400
Neithhotep may have ruled, at least for a
short while, as pharaohs in their own right.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.180
The first one on whom we have pretty solid
evidence is Sobekneferu.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:18.870
After her brother, Amenemhat IV, died without
a male heir, she took the throne and ruled

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:20.730
for almost four years.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:26.120
Statues and inscriptions of Sobekneferu depict
her with the full royal regalia and titles

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.120
of a pharaoh.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.519
Like her brother, she also did not have any
heirs so her death also brought the end of

00:03:31.519 --> 00:03:34.010
the 12th Dynasty.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:38.900
Her Rise to Power

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:45.439
Hatshepsut was the daughter of Pharaoh Tuthmosis
I and his Great Royal Wife, Ahmose.

00:03:45.439 --> 00:03:49.320
It was common for pharaohs to have multiple
wives, but only one was the main wife who

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:53.500
had certain authority and performed functions
we would associate with a queen.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:58.510
Normally, the heir of the pharaoh was also
the son of the Great Royal Wife, but this

00:03:58.510 --> 00:03:59.590
was not always the case.

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:02.150
Indeed, that was not what we had here.

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:07.249
Tuthmosis II succeeded to the throne after
his father’s death circa 1493 BC, but he

00:04:07.249 --> 00:04:12.230
was not the son of Ahmose, but rather a secondary
wife called Mutnofret.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:17.650
The new pharaoh married his stepsister Hatshepsut
when she was about 12 years old.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:20.859
They only had one child that we know of, a
daughter called Neferure.

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:25.080
The pharaoh’s other known child was his
heir, Tuthmosis III, whom he had with his

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.690
secondary wife Iset.

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:32.470
The reign of Tuthmosis II is one full of questions,
including how long it actually lasted.

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:37.830
The traditional length which comes from ancient
sources is 13 to 14 years, but some modern

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:41.949
Egyptologists argue that it may have been
a full decade shorter.

00:04:41.949 --> 00:04:46.470
Assuming the older version, then the pharaoh
would have died in 1479 BC.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:51.740
Technically, he was succeeded by his son,
Tuthmosis III, but the new king was only two

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:57.340
or three years old so his stepmother, the
Great Royal Wife Hatshepsut, ruled as his

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:58.340
regent.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:03.140
So far, everything went according to protocol,
but Hatshepsut was expected to cede all authority

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:05.090
when Tuthmosis came of age.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:06.199
She didn’t, though.

00:05:06.199 --> 00:05:10.730
Instead, she took the power for herself and
became a fully fledged pharaoh.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:14.400
Unfortunately, we don’t have the complete
details of her rise to the throne.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.701
We don’t know if it was something gradual
as Hatshepsut kept awarding herself more and

00:05:18.701 --> 00:05:22.030
more authority or if she took over in one
fell swoop.

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:26.490
We do know that in the early years of her
regency, she still used traditional titles

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:31.870
such as “King’s Chief Wife” or “God’s
Wife” and was portrayed sitting behind or

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:33.520
to the side of the young pharaoh.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:38.199
However, around the seventh year of Tuthmosis’s
reign, she began being depicted as the King

00:05:38.199 --> 00:05:41.710
of Egypt, with all the titles and regalia
that came with it.

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:44.819
What would have been nice to know is how the
people of Egypt reacted to Hatshepsut’s

00:05:44.819 --> 00:05:45.819
power play.

00:05:45.819 --> 00:05:49.729
She ruled as pharaoh for well over a decade
so, clearly, they weren’t that up in arms

00:05:49.729 --> 00:05:54.360
about it, but that may also be due to the
fact that she took extra steps to gain their

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:55.360
approval.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:59.990
For starters, Hatshepsut established her divine
legacy, which was easy to do since she was

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:05.440
the daughter of Tuthmosis I, and she also
claimed that it was always his will for her

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.530
to rule alongside her stepbrother.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.970
Afterwards, she obtained the support of the
high officials.

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:14.569
How exactly she did this, we don’t know,
probably with bribes and promotions, but we

00:06:14.569 --> 00:06:18.910
do know the name of one man who achieved great
status during her reign.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:24.580
His name was Senenmut and he was, among others,
the Great Treasurer, the Chief Royal Architect,

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:26.720
and the Chief Steward of the King’s Daughter.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:31.360
Because of his swift rise to power, there
has been speculation that he and Hatshepsut

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:34.319
might have been lovers, but there is no proof
of this.

00:06:34.319 --> 00:06:39.009
His true legacy is the mortuary temple that
he designed and built for Hatshepsut which

00:06:39.009 --> 00:06:43.949
remains, even today, among the most recognizable
examples of ancient Egyptian architecture.

00:06:43.949 --> 00:06:48.410
Moreover, there was also the ceiling decoration
of his own tomb which contains a celestial

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:53.129
diagram and represents one of the most detailed
displays of Egyptian astronomy.

00:06:53.129 --> 00:06:57.889
The last step in Hatshepsut’s process to
secure power was the most unusual as it had

00:06:57.889 --> 00:07:02.970
not been employed by any of the other female
pharaohs - Hatshepsut portrayed herself as

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:03.970
a man.

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:08.169
There are statues and inscriptions of her
where she was clearly depicted with a male

00:07:08.169 --> 00:07:11.039
body and described using male pronouns.

00:07:11.039 --> 00:07:15.220
She even had the fake, pointy beard that we
see on a lot of pharaohs.

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:19.789
That being said, this practice wasn’t universal
and we also have statues where Hatshepsut

00:07:19.789 --> 00:07:25.159
is shown as a woman, as well as inscriptions
where she used feminine titles such as the

00:07:25.159 --> 00:07:27.879
“Female Horus” or “Daughter of Ra.”

00:07:27.879 --> 00:07:30.583
Perhaps once she felt that her position was
secure, she abandoned the custom.

00:07:30.583 --> 00:07:35.729
The Reign of the Pharaoh

00:07:35.729 --> 00:07:41.919
Now we move on to Hatshepsut’s motivation
for her power grab.

00:07:41.919 --> 00:07:45.280
Many historians believe she did it purely
to satisfy her ambition.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:50.110
In fact, when Hatshepsut’s story was first
being unraveled about a century ago, she was

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:52.810
portrayed in a very negative light.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:56.770
Scholars of that time accused her of acts
of deception and deviant behavior.

00:07:56.770 --> 00:08:01.849
They called her the “vilest type of usurper”
and a “vain, ambitious, and unscrupulous

00:08:01.849 --> 00:08:02.849
woman.”

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:07.509
They said that her husband, Tuthmosis II,
was a frail and weak ruler whom she easily

00:08:07.509 --> 00:08:12.979
pushed around and manipulated while the scheming
Senenmut was her co-conspirator and lover.

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:17.300
Meanwhile, Tuthmosis III grew up loathing
his stepmother and took revenge on her after

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:19.830
she died by trying to erase her name from
history.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:24.939
It’s a compelling story where Hatshepsut
portrays the villain, but one that has fallen

00:08:24.939 --> 00:08:30.039
out of favor with today’s scholars who consider
it based more on the preconceptions of the

00:08:30.039 --> 00:08:32.390
time rather than hard proof.

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:36.560
While it is possible, even likely, that it
was Hatshepsut’s desire for power which

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:43.219
determined her actions, to say that she was
a deviant, ruthless usurper is a tad too far.

00:08:43.219 --> 00:08:47.970
After all, if she was really so cold-blooded,
she would have found a way to get rid of her

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:52.690
stepson since he was the biggest threat to
her power, especially after he came of age,

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:54.140
but she never did.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.120
She didn’t threaten or persecute him.

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:58.220
Tuthmosis was allowed to do as he pleased.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:01.600
He first trained as a soldier and then became
the commander of the Egyptian army.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:06.930
In fact, some historians opine that Hatshepsut
did exactly the opposite, that she took power

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:12.130
during a political crisis to protect herself
and Tuthmosis from another pretender to the

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:14.720
throne from a different branch of the royal
family.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:18.990
Once she did it, it was a lifetime gig so
she couldn’t surrender power even if she

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:19.990
wanted to.

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:24.170
Or maybe she was telling the truth all along
when she said that her father wanted her to

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:25.779
succeed as pharaoh.

00:09:25.779 --> 00:09:28.490
It’s unlikely, but not impossible.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:31.940
Motivations aside, let’s talk about Hatshepsut’s
actual reign.

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:33.580
Was it any good?

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:38.399
Well, in fact, it was, and historians describe
it as a period of prosperity for Egypt.

00:09:38.399 --> 00:09:40.350
It was also relatively peaceful.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:45.230
There is mention of some military expeditions,
but we don’t know any significant details.

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:49.800
Because they were so poorly documented, scholars
opine that Hatshepsut did it more out of a

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:54.620
sense of obligation, feeling that it was her
duty to continue the conquests of her father.

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:59.930
The true triumphs of Hatshepsut’s reign
as pharaoh came from trade and construction.

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:03.940
According to records, she re-established a
network with one of their former trade partners

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:07.810
during the Middle Kingdom, a mysterious land
known as Punt.

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:12.810
An ancient inscription says that the pharaoh’s
ships returned filled with ebony, ivory, myrrh,

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:16.519
cinnamon wood, and the “green gold of Emu”
aka electrum.

00:10:16.519 --> 00:10:19.940
Nowadays, we’re not even sure where Punt
was exactly.

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:24.940
Best guesses suggest southeast of Egypt along
the Red Sea coast over parts of modern countries

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:28.160
such as Somalia, Eritrea, and Sudan.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.230
Hatshepsut was also a prolific builder.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:35.450
The age of the pyramids might have been over,
but she wanted to show that Egypt was still

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:37.820
capable of grand constructions.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:40.660
She started off with monuments at the Temple
of Karnak.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:46.170
There, she erected twin 100-ft tall obelisks
which, at the time, were the tallest in the

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:47.170
world.

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:50.390
She was clearly a fan of these giant stone
pillars as she ordered the construction of

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:52.230
several more throughout her reign.

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:56.470
The biggest of them all cracked while it was
being carved and it was simply abandoned in

00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:57.700
its quarry in Aswan.

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:02.779
It has since become known as the Unfinished
Obelisk and not only is it still there today,

00:11:02.779 --> 00:11:07.130
but it has provided us with unique insight
into how the ancient Egyptian stone workers

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:08.380
built these monuments.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:13.269
Of course, Hatshepsut’s masterpiece was
her mortuary temple, the Djeser-Djeseru or

00:11:13.269 --> 00:11:14.550
“Holy of Holies.”

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:17.660
It had three levels decorated with reliefs
and statues.

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:21.240
Each level had colonnades which told of Hatshepsut’s
triumphs.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:25.610
The Birth Colonnade, for example, presented
her divine creation and how the god Amun was

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:27.010
her true father.

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:32.029
The Punt Colonnade, as you might expect, detailed
how she, once again, brought riches from the

00:11:32.029 --> 00:11:33.880
Land of Punt back to Egypt.

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:36.760
There were several temples and chapels dedicated
to the gods.

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:41.990
There were also several tombs located in proximity,
such as that of Senenmut, the chief architect

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:43.170
of her mortuary temple.

00:11:43.170 --> 00:12:17.820
For unknown reasons, he was never placed inside
his tomb and we do not know where he is buried.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:26.149
Speaking of which...

00:12:26.149 --> 00:12:53.870
Where Is Hatshepsut’s Mummy?

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:59.029
As with many pharaohs, the story of Hatshepsut
has continued into modern times thanks to

00:12:59.029 --> 00:13:00.260
archaeologists finding her tomb.

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:05.040
However, her contemporary tale is a long and
complicated one that stands out from the rest

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:07.630
because it has been developing for over 200
years.

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:14.040
Hatshepsut died in 1458 BC and was buried
in a royal tomb dubbed KV20, originally alongside

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:16.200
her father, Thutmosis I.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:20.570
The tomb itself is notable because Egyptologists
believe it may have been the first of its

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:24.410
kind built in the region known today as the
Valley of the Kings.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:28.980
The first mention we have of the tomb in modern
times actually comes to us courtesy of Napoleon.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:34.470
Maybe not him personally, but the scientific
expedition he sent there in 1799.

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:38.779
It was part of his campaign into Egypt and
Syria which mostly consisted of him fighting

00:13:38.779 --> 00:13:41.320
the Ottoman and the English Empires.

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:45.821
On the military side, the campaign was a flop
- Napoleon failed to conquer Syria and, while

00:13:45.821 --> 00:13:50.310
he did capture Egypt, he later had to surrender
it in order to withdraw.

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:54.730
On the scientific side, however, it turned
out to be one of the most significant expeditions

00:13:54.730 --> 00:13:55.760
in history.

00:13:55.760 --> 00:14:01.880
A large contingency of 167 engineers and scientists
from all areas of study traveled to Egypt.

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:04.970
They mapped the area and recovered thousands
of items.

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:09.899
Of particular importance was the discovery
of one artifact by a young engineer named

00:14:09.899 --> 00:14:15.649
Pierre-François Bouchard - an artifact without
which we would have never been able to understand

00:14:15.649 --> 00:14:21.380
the language, traditions, and history of the
Egyptians - the Rosetta Stone.

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:26.130
These discoveries triggered renewed interest
in ancient Egypt back in Europe and, more

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:29.350
or less, helped establish the field of Egyptology.

00:14:29.350 --> 00:14:34.279
As far as KV20 was concerned, the French found
it and mapped it, but it was buried under

00:14:34.279 --> 00:14:36.670
rubble so they did not explore it.

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:43.130
In 1817, pioneering Italian Egyptologist Giovanni
Battista Belzoni also made note of the tomb’s

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:48.610
location but, like before, he did not have
the time, resources, or disposition to excavate

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:49.610
it.

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:53.110
By the way, that Belzoni was quite a character
and lived a fascinating life, one you can

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:56.880
learn about in detail in the video we did
on him a while back.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:01.800
Getting back to Hatshepsut, it wasn’t until
1825 that an attempt was made to excavate

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:04.880
her tomb by British scholar James Burton.

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:07.800
He only made it as far as the first chamber.

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:13.920
Afterwards came German Egyptologist Karl Richard
Lepsius in 1844 who mapped out parts of the

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:17.480
tomb but, again, failed to make any significant
progress.

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:22.670
It wasn’t until 1904 that someone finally
managed to clear out all the debris and completely

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:27.890
excavate KV20 and that someone was Howard
Carter, the same archaeologist who would discover

00:15:27.890 --> 00:15:31.490
the tomb of Tutankhamun 18 years later.

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:35.190
Carter completely cleaned out the tomb of
all its contents, but afterwards came the

00:15:35.190 --> 00:15:38.310
hard part of trying to make sense of what
he found.

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:42.490
There were quite a few artifacts which had
inscriptions mentioning Hatshepsut as the

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:47.450
“King of Upper and Lower Egypt,” cementing
the idea that she served as pharaoh in her

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:49.500
own right and not just as regent.

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:56.380
However, what puzzled Egyptologists was the
presence of two sarcophagi...but no mummies.

00:15:56.380 --> 00:16:00.700
Both had been built for Hatshepsut, yet neither
one contained any remains.

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:05.790
The mystery of why there were two coffins
was solved relatively easily when archaeologists

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:07.120
translated the hieroglyphics.

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:10.740
Part of the inscription said “ ...long live
the Female Horus...

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:12.399
Hatchepsut-Khnemet-Amun!

00:16:12.399 --> 00:16:13.699
May she live forever!

00:16:13.699 --> 00:16:18.920
She made it as her monument to her father
whom she loved...Thutmosis the justified.”

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:23.760
Basically, the first sarcophagus had been
built for Hatshepsut, but then she refurbished

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.680
it for her father so that they would be buried
together.

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:31.700
However, Tuthmosis I was later removed from
the sarcophagus and placed inside another

00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:33.209
tomb, KV38.

00:16:33.209 --> 00:16:36.870
This was supposed to be a grander mausoleum
more befitting someone of his stature, but

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:39.800
this tomb was discovered and completely plundered
by looters.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:44.199
That still left one big question: What happened
to Hatshepsut?

00:16:44.199 --> 00:16:48.800
Her remains were probably removed alongside
those of her father, but where were they?

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:51.880
Over the decades, several potential candidates
were put forward.

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:56.829
Hatshepsut became similar to Nefertiti, another
famous Egyptian queen whose remains have yet

00:16:56.829 --> 00:16:57.870
to be found.

00:16:57.870 --> 00:17:02.470
Every time a new female mummy was discovered
that was about the right age, people speculated

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:04.420
that she might be the long-lost pharaoh.

00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:10.380
One of those candidates was a nameless mummy
discovered in 1903, also by Howard Carter,

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:11.870
in Tomb KV60.

00:17:11.870 --> 00:17:16.450
This was a modest tomb which belonged to Sitre
In, a woman who held the title of royal wet

00:17:16.450 --> 00:17:17.940
nurse to Hatshepsut.

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:23.260
Her remains were also located inside the crypt
so this begged the question of who was the

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:24.930
second mummy?

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:29.150
Egyptologist Elizabeth Thomas was the first
to speculate in the 1960s that the unidentified

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:30.570
mummy might be Hatshepsut.

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:34.310
Back then, however, technology wasn’t ready
yet to support her hypothesis.

00:17:34.310 --> 00:17:39.210
It wasn’t until last decade that we finally
got some evidence to back up the idea.

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:44.470
Besides the mummy, archaeologists had previously
recovered other items belonging to Hatshepsut

00:17:44.470 --> 00:17:46.220
from her mortuary temple.

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:50.690
Among them was a wooden box with her name
on it which contained a preserved molar purported

00:17:50.690 --> 00:17:51.910
to be that of the pharaoh.

00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:56.630
In 2007, forensic orthodontists took the tooth
and compared it to several mummies believed

00:17:56.630 --> 00:17:59.030
to be Hatshepsut to see if any of them matched.

00:17:59.030 --> 00:18:04.190
Indeed, the one found in KV60 fit the bill
exactly, prompting many scholars to proclaim

00:18:04.190 --> 00:18:07.310
that, at long last, Hatshepsut had been found.

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:11.780
To strengthen their argument, DNA tests also
showed that the mummy shared genetic similarities

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:15.720
to Ahmose-Nefertari, the first queen of the
18th Dynasty.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:20.100
That’s not conclusive, but it did show that
they were related and, therefore, the KV60

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:21.920
mummy came from a royal bloodline.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:26.300
This proof was enough for many Egyptologists,
although plenty of others remain skeptical

00:18:26.300 --> 00:18:28.270
to this day.

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:31.350
Death &amp; Disappearance

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:37.380
Let’s assume, like many others did, that
the mummy found in KV60 is, indeed, Hatshepsut.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:41.860
In that case, we can also start talking about
her cause of death.

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:46.040
Ancient sources are quiet on the subject as
the pharaoh simply disappeared from the historical

00:18:46.040 --> 00:18:51.040
record, although it is generally presumed
that she died in her 22nd regnal year.

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:54.950
If the mummy is Hatshepsut, then she wasn’t
exactly the peak of good health.

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:56.240
Shocking, I know.

00:18:56.240 --> 00:19:00.150
The pharaoh was an overweight middle-aged
woman with arthritis, bad skin, bad teeth,

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:04.140
she may have suffered from diabetes and probably
died of skin cancer.

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:08.690
Worst of all, she may have given herself cancer
by using a carcinogenic skin treatment.

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:12.760
In the same place that archaeologists found
the wooden box with Hatshepsut’s tooth,

00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:14.270
they also recovered a flask.

00:19:14.270 --> 00:19:18.431
At first, they thought it originally contained
perfume, but later they concluded that it

00:19:18.431 --> 00:19:22.990
was some sort of lotion used to treat a skin
condition like psoriasis or eczema.

00:19:22.990 --> 00:19:27.490
It contained traces of harmless ingredients
such as oils and fatty acids, but also a tar

00:19:27.490 --> 00:19:29.310
residue that could cause cancer.

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:33.590
It’s impossible to say how long the pharaoh
used the lotion for, but it is definitely

00:19:33.590 --> 00:19:39.320
possible that she inadvertently poisoned herself
to alleviate the symptoms of her skin disease.

00:19:39.320 --> 00:19:43.370
That could potentially solve the riddle of
Hatshepsut’s death, but there is still the

00:19:43.370 --> 00:19:45.240
mystery of her disappearance.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:50.360
By all accounts, Hatshepsut was condemned
to damnatio memoriae after death, the process

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:53.900
where her name and her actions were removed
from the historical record to try and make

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:55.860
it look like she never existed.

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:58.130
The question is “Why?”

00:19:58.130 --> 00:20:01.740
In this regard, scholars only have ideas and
speculations.

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:07.390
Many have pointed the finger at the obvious
suspect, her stepson, Tuthmosis III, or possibly

00:20:07.390 --> 00:20:11.540
his son and successor, Amenhotep II (or even
both of them).

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:12.540
The motives were varied.

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:17.730
We have no document or artifact to suggest
how Tuthmosis felt about his stepmother, but

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:22.220
he could have resented her for, basically,
usurping his power when she was just supposed

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:23.280
to rule as regent.

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:26.720
He also could have wanted to eliminate her
reign in order to create a more direct lineage

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:31.210
to his father and grandfather, Tuthmosis II
and Tuthmosis I.

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:35.920
If that’s the case, though, the damnatio
memoriae was more likely ordered by Amenhotep

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:37.160
than his father.

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:41.530
Tuthmosis III became a great pharaoh in his
own right and his claim to the throne was

00:20:41.530 --> 00:20:42.530
unquestionable.

00:20:42.530 --> 00:20:47.230
In fact, he was ancient Egypt’s most successful
military ruler and created the largest Egyptian

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:51.350
empire in history and he is also someone you
will probably see on this channel in the near

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:52.350
future.

00:20:52.350 --> 00:20:56.460
Amenhotep II, on the other hand, was not his
father’s first choice as successor.

00:20:56.460 --> 00:21:01.370
He was not his firstborn son, nor was he given
birth by Tuthmosis’s Great Royal Wife, Satiah.

00:21:01.370 --> 00:21:06.650
However, the original heir, Prince Amenemhat,
and his mother both died, thus allowing Amenhotep

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:08.310
to take the throne.

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:11.730
The pharaoh could have felt that his royal
claim was not as strong as it could be, so

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:16.840
he sought out to create a direct lineage to
the first rulers of the 18th dynasty and eliminate

00:21:16.840 --> 00:21:19.750
outliers, by which we mean Hatshepsut.

00:21:19.750 --> 00:21:23.930
Or it could be that the motive was far less
personal and it simply represented a return

00:21:23.930 --> 00:21:24.930
to tradition.

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:28.770
Hatshepsut’s reign, even though it was successful
and helped the empire prosper, went against

00:21:28.770 --> 00:21:29.770
the norm.

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:33.830
Some argue that it was a problem exactly because
it was successful, as it showed Egyptians

00:21:33.830 --> 00:21:38.450
that women could make good pharaohs, too,
and they couldn’t have that, could they?

00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:42.821
Whatever the reason might have been and whoever
may have wanted Hatshepsut erased from the

00:21:42.821 --> 00:21:47.570
history books, the important thing is that
they did not do a very good job.

00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:51.690
Most of the small artifacts that had her image
or inscriptions with her name were left alone.

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:56.230
Even the bigger stuff like statues or stelae
were just smashed up and buried outside her

00:21:56.230 --> 00:21:57.230
temple.

00:21:57.230 --> 00:22:00.400
When archaeologists excavated the area in
the 19th and 20th centuries, they were able

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:06.560
to recover enough information to restore Hatshepsut’s
name and allow her to, once again, take her

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:26.590
rightful place as one of the greatest pharaohs
in history.

